tt-toplike (0.9.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Direct (non-Docker) vLLM-on-TT detection for the [i] Inference Server
    panel. Previously the panel only saw a `docker run`-launched
    tt-inference-server container; a bare `vllm serve <model> ...` or
    `server_example_tt.py --model <model> ...` process (as `tt-model serve`
    from tt-tnt launches directly) was invisible to it, surfacing only as a
    generic "vllm" process in the plain process table.
    - New `Source::Host { pid }` alongside the existing `Source::Docker`,
      keyed by `service_key()` — `"host-vllm-<pid>"` for a bare process, the
      container name for Docker — used everywhere identity/dedup/prev-state
      lookup happens.
    - `parse_direct_vllm` recognizes both launch shapes and requires
      `MESH_DEVICE` or `TT_METAL_HOME` present in the process's own
      environment before treating it as TT-backed, so a plain upstream vLLM
      process (e.g. against a GPU, for comparison) is never misclassified.
      Recognizes `--port`/`--port=N` and both `vllm serve <model>` and
      `vllm serve --model <model>` forms.
    - New `SystemProbe` wraps the existing `DockerProbe` and dispatches every
      probe call by the identity key's shape: a host key reads `/proc`
      directly and runs local `ps`/`sh` scoped to the launched pid's whole
      process tree (a new `process_tree_pids` walker, since a bare process
      has no cgroup/PID-namespace boundary the way a Docker container does)
      instead of shelling into a container; anything else forwards unchanged
      to the wrapped `DockerProbe`, so the Docker path's behavior is
      untouched byte-for-byte.
    - Host-keyed liveness doesn't rely on matching a process name in `ps`'s
      `comm` column (a pip console-script's `comm` is the script's own name,
      not `python3`, so the existing Docker-calibrated heuristic would have
      read a compiling/loading `vllm serve` as `Down` for the entire
      90-minute silent window this feature exists to illuminate) — a
      host-keyed sample is "alive" whenever its tree-scoped `ps` returns any
      row at all, since that invocation is already scoped to exactly the
      right pids.
    - `host_exec` (the shell used for the kernel/weight-shard `find` probes)
      allowlists only `TT_METAL_HOME`/`CACHE_ROOT`/`HOME`/`PATH` from the
      target process's environment rather than copying it wholesale — this
      box is shared between users, and copying an arbitrary local process's
      full environment into a root-run monitor's spawned shell (e.g. via
      `LD_PRELOAD`) would have been a real privilege-escalation surface.
    - Everything downstream (phase detection, progress estimation, vLLM
      `/metrics` parsing, panel rendering) needed no changes — it already
      operated on the backend-agnostic `ServiceState`/`TickSample` shapes.
    Design: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-27-direct-vllm-detection-design.md.
    Not hardware-verified against a real `tt-model serve` launch as of this
    release — see that spec's manual-verification checklist.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Thu, 27 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

tt-toplike (0.8.0) noble; urgency=medium

  * Telemetry expansion — the sysfs/hybrid path picks up most of what used
    to require tt-smi or Luwen, four work items:
    - Sysfs backend now reads label-aware hwmon sensors (matches `*_label`
      instead of assuming a fixed `tempN`/`inN`/`currN` index), a fan
      sensor, and real per-board `*_max` limit files (e.g. 125 W / 500 A /
      90 °C on p300c) instead of hardcoded guesses. It also discovers
      tt-kmd's class-attr directory
      (`/sys/class/tenstorrent/tenstorrent!N/`) via the hwmon `device`
      symlink and reads it two ways: once at startup for static enrichment
      (`tt_card_type` → the real board SKU, `tt_fw_bundle_ver`) — which
      *replaces* the old ~1.2 s tt-smi startup probe on modern kmd — and
      once per tick for dynamic attributes (`tt_aiclk`, `tt_heartbeat`,
      previously hardcoded to `None` in this backend) plus a synthesized
      partial `SmbusTelemetry` (clocks, fan, thermal trips, serial), so
      sysfs is no longer SMBUS-blind the way it was before.
    - New `pcie_counters` module reads tt-kmd's twelve
      `pcie_perf_counters/` data-word counters, folds them into in/out
      directions, and derives live bandwidth (words × 4 bytes / dt) with
      reset-clamping for counter wraparound. Exposed via a defaulted
      `TelemetryBackend::pcie_bandwidth()` trait method implemented by the
      sysfs and hybrid backends; Insights shows it as a two-row PCIe panel
      with link geometry (GenN ×width) and live ▼/▲ rates.
    - JSON backend parses tt-smi's full `board_info` (PCIe speed/width,
      tolerant of tt-smi's number-vs-string drift across versions) and
      handles tt-smi 5.3.0's fan-semantics change by choosing whichever of
      FAN_SPEED / FAN_RPM holds a real (non-sentinel) RPM — live tt-smi
      emits *both* keys, and on Blackhole FAN_SPEED is the `0x0` sentinel
      while FAN_RPM carries the reading, so a present-but-absent fallback
      chain would still have left the Fan row blank. It also picks up
      tt-smi 6.x's top-level `processes[]` array
      (new `DeviceProcess` model + `device_processes()` trait method,
      wired through both the JSON and WebSocket backends) and
      `telemetry.board_power`.
    - Insights sidebar gained Current (vs. TDC limit), Board power, PCIe
      (link geometry + live bandwidth), GDDR ECC (uncorrectable counts
      emphasized in red), and thermal-trip rows; the hybrid backend
      propagates PCIe link geometry and board power via a new `DeviceMeta`
      struct, and `format_bandwidth` is hardened with a provable ≤9-char
      width bound so the panel never wraps.
    - Both Prometheus scrapers (vLLM + media-server) now accept either
      KV-cache metric name a given vLLM build emits
      (`gpu_cache_usage_perc` or `kv_cache_usage_perc`) via a shared
      `parse_kv_cache_line` helper — some deployments only expose one or
      the other.
  * Cross-backend limit parity (found by diffing a luwen build against the
    default hybrid build on the same 4× Blackhole box — two of this
    release's headline features weren't reaching two of the four backends):
    - `--backend json` never parsed tt-smi's `limits` block at all: tt-smi
      emits those numbers as quoted strings (`"tdp_limit": "125"`) and
      serde fails the whole struct on one bad field, so `Device::limits`
      stayed `None` and the pure-JSON path fell back to the generic
      300 W / 105 °C these limits were meant to replace. The hybrid path
      masked the bug because hwmon `*_max` supplies its limits instead.
      `DeviceLimits` now deserializes through a tolerant intermediate
      (number-or-string, shared with the rest of the tt-smi parser via a
      new `models::serde_num`).
    - The displayed thermal limit is now the ~90 °C *throttle* trip point
      on every backend. tt-smi's `thm_limit` is the harder 110 °C shutdown
      trip; its `therm_trip_l1_limit` (90 °C) is what hwmon publishes as
      `temp1_max`, so the JSON path is switched to prefer it and only falls
      back to `thm_limit` for pre-5.3.0 snapshots.
    - luwen backend populated neither `Device::limits` nor
      `Device::firmwares` (both were hardcoded `None`), so its Insights
      rows showed `(lim 105°C)` against the safe backend's `(lim 90°C)`,
      dropped the Current row's `(lim 500A)` entirely, and rendered the FW
      row as `—`. Both are now mapped from the telemetry read that detection
      already performs for the board type (no extra hardware touch), using
      `thm_limit_throttle` for consistency with hwmon and a new
      `fw_bundle_version_string` decode of the packed FLASH_BUNDLE_VERSION
      register. Limits are Blackhole-only — those registers are BH telemetry
      tags Wormhole never assigns, so they stay absent there rather than
      reporting a fabricated "(lim 0W)". The PCIe row remains legitimately
      absent on luwen (no link speed/width source) and is now documented as
      such rather than faked.
  * Device ordering fix (affects every multi-card box): the sysfs backend
    assigned device indices in raw `readdir` order, which is neither hwmon
    number nor PCI bus-id order and is not stable across boots. The hybrid
    backend joins tt-smi metadata and SMBUS telemetry by *index*, and
    `tt-smi -s` orders devices ascending by bus id — so on a 4-card box
    every device displayed another card's DDR/ARC/GDDR/ECC/fan/clock data
    plus this release's new PCIe geometry and board power. Discovery is now
    sorted by bus id before indices are assigned.
  * luwen-backend migrated off the abandoned `all-smi-luwen-*` forks (13
    months without a release) onto the official
    `luwen-api`/`luwen-pci`/`luwen-def` 0.8.5 crates. Picks up Blackhole
    GDDR temperatures/ECC counters, harvesting/enabled core masks, thermal
    trips, and input/board power-limit fields the forked crates never
    exposed. `luwen-pci` is required, not optional: `luwen-api` carries no
    hardware transport, so an early cut of this migration could not detect
    a device at all — enumeration now goes through
    `luwen_pci::detect_chips_silent`, which does its own PCI scan.
    **Verified on 4x Blackhole p300c (tt-kmd 2.9.0, idle):** all four chips
    detected, and power, current, ASIC/GDDR temperatures and ETH link state
    agree with the sysfs/hybrid path and `tt-smi -s`. Wormhole and Grayskull
    are still unverified — no such silicon was available, so their register
    layouts rest on unit tests over hand-built telemetry, with arch gating
    to stop Blackhole-only tags being published as a confident zero on
    Wormhole. Behaviour under load is likewise untested. The backend
    remains launch-only (never auto-detected, never touched by the live `b`
    backend-cycle key) pending upstream arbitration between Luwen's BAR0
    mapping and a tt-kmd already serving an active workload. Expect slower
    startup than sysfs (a PCI scan plus a scratch-register read per chip)
    and no PCIe row, since the link counters and geometry have no luwen
    source and are left absent rather than fabricated.

 -- Taylor Singletary <tsingletary@tenstorrent.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

tt-toplike (0.7.30) noble; urgency=medium

  * Startup latency (under load): the Sysfs backend's board-name enrichment
    shelled `tt-smi --version` + `tt-smi -s` synchronously during init, with no
    timeout. While a model is compiling/loading, tt-smi contends with the
    workload for ARC/CPU and can take several seconds per call — so starting
    tt-toplike during a load stalled the first frame by ~10 s. The enrichment
    now runs on a helper thread bounded to 1.2 s; on timeout it's skipped and
    the device keeps its hwmon arch name (same graceful degradation as when
    tt-smi is absent), so the TUI paints promptly regardless of load. Idle
    behavior is unchanged (the probe completes well under the cap). The parse
    is now a pure, unit-tested helper.

 -- Tenstorrent <software@tenstorrent.com>  Wed, 08 Jul 2026 14:00:00 +0000

tt-toplike (0.7.29) noble; urgency=medium

  * Address PR #20 review (ezietlow), inference-server detection hardening:
    - detect: `--publish` parsing now takes the HOST port (the side the monitor
      probes), not the container port — an asymmetric `0.0.0.0:8000:8001` mapping
      was probing 8001 and reading Down.
    - monitor: when a container is seen by both the host-process scan and the
      docker-inspect path, prefer the record with a resolved host port so a
      foreground `docker run` with no `--publish` isn't probed at the default 8000.
    - probe: `docker()` now caps stdout at 8 MiB and kills the child on timeout,
      so a huge `find` output or a wedged `docker exec` can't leak a thread or
      balloon memory each tick.
    - metrics: Prometheus value parsing takes the value field explicitly, so an
      optional trailing timestamp is no longer parsed as the value (latent).
    - liveness: the `host:port` cmdline fallback only fires on host-looking
      tokens (dotted IP / localhost, or a `--host`/`--url` value), so a
      `--served-model-name foo:v2` no longer yields a bogus port.
    +tests for each (asymmetric publish, port-preferring dedup, timestamped
    metric line, non-host token rejection).

