tt-toplike (0.7.23) noble; urgency=medium

  * --serve [BIND:PORT] + in-app /serve command: tt-toplike can now BE a
    telemetry source, not just consume one. It runs a WebSocket server on
    /telemetry broadcasting frames that are valid `tt-smi -s` JSON plus an
    optional additive `tt_toplike` key carrying the box's process list and
    [i] inference state — the same shape tt-station-agentd emits. Any
    tt-toplike --remote can connect to it directly, so the remote data
    source is no longer a single agent. Serves while you watch (at a TTY the
    TUI runs and publishes in one process) or headless (no TTY → a quiet
    collector loop). Default bind 0.0.0.0:8770; plaintext/unauthed,
    trusted-LAN posture matching --remote. Requires the json or hybrid
    backend (the only ones that retain a raw tt-smi frame to relay).
  * --remote now renders the WATCHED box fully: the process panel shows the
    publisher's processes and the [i] tab shows its inference workload, both
    read from the streamed `tt_toplike` extension. When the stream carries
    only chip telemetry (e.g. an agent that hasn't adopted the extension),
    the panels fall back to the local machine's data with an honest LOCAL
    label — no more silently showing your laptop's processes as if they were
    the box's. A missing extension sub-key means "not streamed → fall back",
    a present-but-empty one means "authoritatively zero".
  * Relay (--serve together with --remote): the origin box's frame is
    re-broadcast verbatim, so downstream viewers see the watched box's
    processes/inference rather than the relay host's.
  * All new code is behind the default-on `remote` cargo feature; the crate
    still builds and is clippy-clean with --no-default-features. Panic-free
    on untrusted frames; the WS server, socket binds, and broadcasts run off
    the render thread on the publisher's own Tokio runtime.

